# TEST-1a kernel-config delta: expose a second 8250 UART (COM2 / ttyS1).
#
# Human-readable RECORD of the change (the authoritative config is
# microvm-kernel-x86_64-6.1.config, which build_kernel.sh copies to .config and
# runs through olddefconfig — that strips comments, hence this file, matching the
# step2a-container.config / step3b-mpparse.config convention).
#
# Two-line change vs the Step-3b config:
CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_NR_UARTS=2
CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_RUNTIME_UARTS=2
#
# Rationale:
#   TEST-1a adds a modeled control channel: a 2nd 16550 (COM2, port 0x2f8, IRQ3)
#   in the VMM, driven by a guest-side `tdvmm-agent` that blocks reading ttyS1. The
#   pinned kernel shipped with CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_NR_UARTS=1, which HARD-CAPS the
#   8250 driver to a single registered port — the `8250.nr_uarts=` module param is
#   clamped to CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_NR_UARTS, so ttyS1 could never appear at runtime
#   without a rebuild. Raising both the compile-time cap (NR_UARTS) and the default
#   count (RUNTIME_UARTS) to 2 makes the kernel probe + register the standard COM2
#   legacy port, so /dev/ttyS1 exists and the agent can open it. IRQ3 is already
#   routed identity to IO-APIC pin 3 by the MP table (src/mptable.rs), so no
#   interrupt-routing change was needed.
#
# Reproducibility: the rebuilt vmlinux is a NEW pinned artifact; its sha256 is
# recorded in guest/kernel/vmlinux-6.1.128.sha256 (and guest/manifest.txt). The
# reproducibility unit stays the (vmlinux sha256 + initramfs sha256) pair.
